\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Problem 3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider an unemployed worker with a discount factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 who is searching for a job.
 Employment provides a wage income 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

, while unemployment provides 0 income.
 Job finding probability is 
\begin_inset Formula $p(a_{t}$
\end_inset

), where 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\cdot):\left[0,\infty\right)\rightarrow\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 is an increasing function, and 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the search effort of the worker.
 For simplicity, assume that an employed worder never loses the job, and
 his discounted utility is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{u(w)}{1-\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 An unemployed worker's period utility is 
\begin_inset Formula $u(0)-a_{t}$
\end_inset

, i.e., utility from consuming 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 minus the disutility from searching.
 Carefully rewrite the decision problem as a dynamic programming problem.
 Please be precise about the state variable and the control variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
\begin_inset Box Frameless
position "t"
hor_pos "c"
has_inner_box 1
inner_pos "t"
use_parbox 0
use_makebox 0
width "100col%"
special "none"
height "1in"
height_special "totalheight"
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The state space is 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\left\{ 0,1\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The state variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
1 & \mbox{if worker is employed at time t}\\
0 & \mbox{if worker is unemployed at time t}
\end{array}\right.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Control variable 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the search effort of the worker at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t}\in[0,\infty)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $p(a_{t})$
\end_inset

 is the probability of finding a job at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\cdot):\left[0,\infty\right)\rightarrow\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 is an increasing function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The per-period return function is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $r(x_{t},a_{t})=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
u(w) & \mbox{if}\ x_{t}=1\\
u(0)-a_{t} & \mbox{if}\ x_{t}=0
\end{array}\right.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The functional equations are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $V(1)=r(1,a)+\beta V(1)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $V(0)=\begin{array}{c}
\mbox{max}\\
a\ge0
\end{array}\left\{ r(0,a)+\beta\left[p(a)V(1)+(1-p(a))V(0)\right]\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Or, from Sargent Chapter 4.3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V(S)=\left[\begin{array}{c}
V_{1}(1)\\
V_{2}(0)
\end{array}\right]=\max\left[\begin{array}{c}
u(w)\\
u(0)-a
\end{array}\right]+\beta P_{2\times2}\left[\begin{array}{c}
V_{1}(1)\\
V_{2}(0)
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{2\times2}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0\\
p(a) & 1-p(a)
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

